<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What is the difference between cellulitis and a facial abscess?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Cellulitis is a diffuse, spreading bacterial infection through skin layers — it has poorly defined borders and no fluid collection. An abscess is a walled-off collection of pus — it has a well-defined raised border and often feels fluctuantly fluid. Antibiotics alone treat cellulitis; surgical drainage is required for an abscess. Ultrasound or CT can confirm which you have.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-start p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary flex-wrap gap-2">Can facial redness and swelling spread to my eye or brain?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes. Facial infections can spread upward to involve the periorbital tissues and orbit (orbital cellulitis), or downward into oral/floor-of-mouth spaces. In rare cases, cavernous sinus thrombosis occurs — a life-threatening blood clot behind the eyes. Prompt treatment prevents these complications.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Will facial redness go away on its own with antibiotics?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">For uncomplicated cellulitis, antibiotics usually resolve redness within 7–14 days. However, if there is an underlying abscess or dental source not treated, the infection will recur. Our surgeons ensure the source is identified and addressed alongside antibiotic therapy.</div>
</details>
</div>
